#### Technical Details

The `DealVault` supports multiple ERC20 tokens through the `tokens` array and explicitly documents that all supported tokens must use the same number of decimals:

```solidity
/// @dev Every token in `_tokens` must use the same number of decimals
```

However, `initialize()` does not validate this invariant before storing the provided tokens:

```solidity
if (_tokens.length == 0) revert ErrInvalidToken();
tokens = _tokens;
```

This is important because the vault aggregates the raw token amounts without performing any decimal normalization:

```solidity
function _getRaisedAmount(uint96 _trancheMask) internal view returns (uint256 raisedAmount) {
    uint256 tokenCount_ = tokens.length;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenCount_; i++) {
        raisedAmount += trancheAmountRaised[_trancheMask][tokens[i]];
    }
}
```

#### Impact

Informational.

#### Recommendation

Add to the vault or the factory the following check:

```solidity
uint8 canonicalDecimals = IERC20Metadata(_tokens[0]).decimals();

for (uint256 i = 1; i < _tokens.length; ++i) {
    if (IERC20Metadata(_tokens[i]).decimals() != canonicalDecimals) {
        revert ErrInvalidTokenDecimals();
    }
}
```

#### Developer Response

Mitigated in [PR #320](https://github.com/tenka-fi/10k/pull/320), commit [60d85ad9](https://github.com/tenka-fi/10k/commit/60d85ad97dc6289dc26046d449fdbb86c0504304). `DealVaultFactory.createVault()` now rejects tokens whose decimals differ from the canonical asset. Regression tests cover matching and mismatched decimals.

A compromised FundingVault operator can repeatedly buy DealVault shares from a cooperating KYC holder above net asset value (NAV) and sell them back below NAV. Each round trip transfers value from the FundingVault to the cooperating account even though every Oracle value is correct.

#### Technical Details

`FundingVault` initializes `maxOrderSpread` to 1,000 basis points, or 10%:

```solidity
function initialize(address _owner, address _registry, address _token) external initializer {
    __MMFManager_init(_registry, _owner, _token);
    maxOrderSpread = 1000;
}
```

The same operator can post both bid and ask orders for the FundingVault. `postOrder()` only verifies that each individual price remains within the configured spread:

```solidity
if (_signedOrder.order.orderType == OrderType.BID) {
    if (_signedOrder.order.price > maxOrderSpread + BIPS_BASE) revert ErrOrderPriceExceeded();
    // ...
} else {
    if (_signedOrder.order.price < BIPS_BASE - maxOrderSpread) revert ErrOrderPriceExceeded();
    // ...
}
```

The check permits the FundingVault to buy at 110% of NAV and later sell the same position at 90% of NAV. The MatchingEngine operator also chooses which compatible orders to match. There is no independent authorization of the FundingVault's economic terms and no cumulative loss limit across related trades.

During `PRE_WINDOW`, the FundingVault must be BLP-whitelisted because it is the bid payer. During `SETTLING`, the BLP check does not apply. Moving the engine into `SETTLING` requires the strategic role, which the team plans to place behind a multisig, but that call only opens the settlement window. Once it is open, the normal MatchingEngine operator can execute individual matches without another on-chain multisig approval.

A compromised operator and cooperating KYC holder can therefore perform the following cycle:

1. The FundingVault buys the holder's DealVault shares at the upper spread boundary.
2. The FundingVault sells those shares back at the lower spread boundary.
3. The parties repeat the cycle while the FundingVault has liquidity.

At the default 10% spread, each completed cycle transfers value out of the FundingVault.

#### Recommendation

Enforce price-direction limits for FundingVault orders. A FundingVault bid should not buy DealVault shares above NAV, and a FundingVault ask should not sell them below NAV. This still permits the FundingVault to buy positions at a discount and sell them at NAV or a premium while preventing the demonstrated loss-making round trip.

If the protocol must permit FundingVault purchases above NAV or sales below NAV, enforce an independently authorized cumulative loss budget rather than relying only on the maximum spread for each individual order.

#### Developer Response

The team confirmed that the path is possible and that operator compromise leading to asset theft is in scope. The team plans to place the strategic role that opens `SETTLING` behind a multisig and noted that a FundingVault would need BLP status to buy during `PRE_WINDOW`. Independent monitoring may also trigger emergency mode when unsafe orders are observed.

The remaining question is whether the multisig approves each exact FundingVault match or only opens the ordinary settlement window, after which the normal operator can execute matches without further on-chain approval.

`virtualStakeFor()` mints shares without transferring assets or binding the mint to the Oracle update that represents the off-chain investment. If virtual shares are minted before that update, a Bookbuild allocation processed in the gap receives too many shares and captures value from the existing and virtual investors.

#### Technical Details

The updated `virtualStakeFor()` correctly accepts the off-chain-computed share amount directly, but it only increases supply and mints the shares:

```solidity
function virtualStakeFor(address _user, uint256 _shares, uint96 _trancheMask)
    external
    onlyStrategic
    notEmergency
    kycOrFundingVault(_user)
{
    // ...
    TrancheData storage td = trancheData[_trancheMask];
    td.totalSupply += _shares;
    td.updateAccounting(_user, balanceOf(_user, _trancheMask));
    _mint(_user, _trancheMask, _shares, "");
}
```

The corresponding Oracle update is a separate transaction. Bookbuild allocations call `convertToShares()`, which uses the latest Oracle NAV and the current total supply:

```solidity
function convertToShares(uint256 _assets, uint96 _trancheMask) public view returns (uint256) {
    uint256 cleanNav = _getNonStaleCleanNav(_trancheMask) + _getRaisedAmount(_trancheMask) + 1;
    uint256 totalSupply = trancheData[_trancheMask].totalSupply + 1;
    return Math.mulDiv(_assets, totalSupply, cleanNav, Math.Rounding.Floor);
}
```

If the virtual mint happens first, total supply includes the new virtual shares while NAV does not yet include the off-chain contribution. A Bookbuild bidder entering during that interval is therefore issued shares at an artificially low price.

Starting with 100 shares representing $100, the flow then mints 100 virtual shares for a $100 off-chain investment. A $100 Bookbuild allocation processed before the Oracle update receives approximately 200 shares. After the Oracle is corrected and $300 is distributed, the Bookbuild bidder receives approximately $150 while the original and virtual investors receive approximately $75 each. Publishing the correct Oracle value before Bookbuild processing results in approximately $100 for each investor.

The same ordering issue was reproduced after a normal raise and transition to `ACTIVE`. Because the Oracle permits only one tranche update per week, a corrective update can also be unavailable until the following week.

#### Affected Code

- `packages/smart-contracts/src/DealVault/DealVault.sol`: `virtualStakeFor()`, `convertToShares()`
- `packages/smart-contracts/src/DealVault/DealVaultBookbuild.sol`: `processBids()`
- `packages/smart-contracts/src/Oracle.sol`: tranche NAV checkpoints

#### Impact

Medium. The demonstrated ordering transfers 25% of the original and virtual investors' value to the intervening Bookbuild bidder. The loss is permanent and scales with the affected investments. The likelihood is reduced because the strategic service must call `virtualStakeFor()` before the required Oracle update and Bookbuild processing must occur before correction.

#### Recommendation

Bind every virtual mint to the specific Oracle checkpoint that includes its off-chain contribution. Reject a virtual mint if that checkpoint is missing, stale, or already consumed for incompatible issuance. Prevent Bookbuild and other share-producing allocations from executing while a virtual issuance lacks a valid valuation checkpoint.

#### Developer Response
Acknowledged.
To mitigate that, offchain rules will be set up:

- No new allocations into the affected DealVault tranche from the valuation snapshot until the off-chain mint completes. This includes bookbuild processing and FundingVault staking.
- Once the oracle includes the off-chain investment, mint all corresponding shares before any secondary order matching resumes for that tranche.

`DealVaultBookbuild.processRefunds()` scans every price tick between the highest submitted bid and tick zero, including empty ticks. A bidder can submit at the maximum valid tick and force the operator to send 101 refund transactions before a valid tick-zero bidder is refunded and the round closes.

#### Technical Details

Submitting a bid records its tick as the round's `highestBidTick`:

```solidity
if (_tick * BIPS_INCREMENT > BIPS_MAX) revert ErrInvalidTick();
// ...
if (_tick > bookbuild.highestBidTick) bookbuild.highestBidTick = _tick;
```

`processRefunds()` performs at most `LOOPS` iterations per call. An empty queue still consumes an iteration before the cursor moves down by one tick:

```solidity
uint256 iterations = 0;
uint256 tick = bookbuild.highestBidTick;
while (iterations++ < LOOPS) {
    (Node memory node, uint256 index) = bookbuild.bidQueues[tick].peek();
    address user = node.user;
    if (user == address(0)) {
        if (tick == 0) {
            bookbuild.state = bbState.CLOSED;
            return;
        }
        tick--;
        continue;
    }
    // refund populated node
}
bookbuild.highestBidTick = tick;
```

The maximum valid tick is 5,000, while one call examines only 50 positions. A single high-tick bid therefore makes the refund cursor traverse thousands of empty queues before reaching ordinary bids at tick zero.

With one bid at tick 5,000 and a victim bid at tick zero, the round remains in `REFUNDING` after 100 successful `processRefunds()` transactions, the cursor has only reached tick one, and the victim has received nothing. Transaction 101 finally refunds the victim and closes the round. The high-tick bidder recovers its full bid. With both bids at tick zero, both users are refunded and the round closes in one transaction.

This is distinct from the previously discussed minimum-bid issue. Both bids satisfy the configured minimum amount; the delay results from scanning the sparse price-tick range.

#### Affected Code

- `packages/smart-contracts/src/DealVault/DealVaultBookbuild.sol`: `_commit()`, `processRefunds()`
- `packages/smart-contracts/src/DealVault/Queue.sol`: queue traversal

#### Impact

Low. An attacker can delay refunds and round closure while imposing substantial operational transaction costs. User funds remain in custody and can eventually be recovered by continuing to call `processRefunds()`.

#### Recommendation

Track populated ticks in an ordered structure or store a pointer to the next populated tick. Refund processing should scale with the number of live bids rather than the numeric distance between their ticks.

#### Developer Response


Acknowledged and accepted. With a $10,000 post-premium minimum, a fully allocated maximum-tick bid generates a $50,000 premium; full cancellation instead incurs a $1,500 penalty plus forfeited MMF yield. We accept the additional processing costs and delays, including failed raises where bids are refunded without either charge.

`AgoraHelper.depositStep()` and `redeemStep()` transfer the full requested input from the calling vault to the helper before executing an exact-input Uniswap V3 swap. If the pool reaches the configured price limit without consuming all of that input, the helper accepts the swap based only on its output and leaves the unspent vault funds in the helper contract. Because `swap()` is publicly callable and sends its output to the caller, those residual funds can subsequently be converted for an arbitrary caller.

#### Technical Details

The MMF manager executes the helper steps by delegatecall. Each step therefore begins by transferring the requested input from the manager to the immutable helper address:

```solidity
IERC20(inputToken).safeTransfer(THIS, _amount);
(int256 amount0, int256 amount1) = AgoraHelper(THIS).swap(_amount, zeroForOne);
```

`swap()` uses an almost-unbounded price limit:

```solidity
uint160 sqrtPriceLimitX96 = _zeroForOne ? MIN_SQRT_RATIO + 1 : MAX_SQRT_RATIO - 1;
(amount0, amount1) = IUniswapV3PoolActions(UNISWAP_V3_POOL)
    .swap(msg.sender, _zeroForOne, int256(_amount), sqrtPriceLimitX96, "");
```

An exact-input Uniswap V3 swap may stop at its price limit after consuming less than `_amount`. The positive token delta returned by the pool identifies how much input was actually consumed, but neither helper step validates that delta. `_checkSwap()` instead checks only whether the nominal output is within two basis points of the requested input:

```solidity
if (_receivedAmount < _amount) {
    uint256 maxSlippage = _amount * MAX_BPS_SLIPPAGE / 10000;
    if (_receivedAmount < _amount - maxSlippage) revert ErrSlippageExceeded();
}
```

A partial fill can therefore pass whenever its output satisfies this threshold. The difference between `_amount` and the positive input delta remains at `AgoraHelper`, outside the originating vault's holdings and request accounting.

The residual is exposed because `swap()` has no caller restriction, names `msg.sender` as the pool's recipient, and pays the pool from the helper during the callback:

```solidity
function swap(uint256 _amount, bool _zeroForOne) external returns (int256 amount0, int256 amount1) {
    // ...
    (amount0, amount1) = IUniswapV3PoolActions(UNISWAP_V3_POOL)
        .swap(msg.sender, _zeroForOne, int256(_amount), sqrtPriceLimitX96, "");
}

function uniswapV3SwapCallback(int256 amount0Delta, int256 amount1Delta, bytes calldata) external {
    if (msg.sender != UNISWAP_V3_POOL) revert ErrCallerNotUniswapV3Pool();
    if (amount0Delta > 0) IERC20(TOKEN0).safeTransfer(UNISWAP_V3_POOL, uint256(amount0Delta));
    if (amount1Delta > 0) IERC20(TOKEN1).safeTransfer(UNISWAP_V3_POOL, uint256(amount1Delta));
}
```

After the pool price moves away from the boundary, whether through ordinary rebalancing or deliberate manipulation, an arbitrary account can call `swap()` in the residual token's input direction. The pool then pulls that input from `AgoraHelper`, while sending the resulting output directly to the caller.

#### Impact

Low.

#### Recommendation


In both helper steps, verify that the positive input-side delta returned by the pool equals `_amount`, reverting the entire operation on a partial fill. Alternatively, measure the helper's input-token balance before and after the swap and return any unconsumed amount to the originating vault.

Additionally, restrict `swap()` to authorized MMF managers so any tokens accidentally held by the helper cannot be spent for an arbitrary recipient.

#### Developer Response


Mitigated in [PR #320](https://github.com/tenka-fi/10k/pull/320), commit [79dacfeb](https://github.com/tenka-fi/10k/commit/79dacfebb3bbe6f6fbb8a32d6bc8e527ca3c0e92). After a swap, the helper returns its remaining input-token balance to the caller within the same transaction, covering both deposit and redemption directions. Public `swap()` access remains unchanged as an accepted design choice.

When a bid cannot purchase the entire ask, `MatchingEngine._handleOrder()` rounds the corresponding instrument amount down. If the result is zero, the engine nevertheless consumes the bid's entire remaining asset amount and transfers it to the seller. The ask is reduced by zero and can therefore be reused against additional bids.

#### Technical Details

The partial-fill branch calculates the shares transferred to the buyer as follows:

```solidity
if (assetAmount >= _bidExecution.remainingAmount) {
    _bidExecution.status = OrderStatus.FILLED;
    instrumentAmount = _bidExecution.remainingAmount * instrumentAmount / assetAmount;
    assetAmount = _bidExecution.remainingAmount;
    _bidExecution.remainingAmount = 0;
    _askExecution.remainingAmount -= instrumentAmount;
}
```

The division can produce `instrumentAmount == 0`. Despite this, the bid is marked filled and `_processAccounting()` transfers `assetAmount` from the buyer to the seller. Since zero is subtracted from the ask, the seller's same share balance and order remain available for further matches.

Minimum order sizes do not prevent the attack. An attacker can first post an above-minimum MasterVault ask and use an ordinary partial match to leave one raw share unit outstanding. After victims post and fully fund above-minimum bids, the attacker can directly donate assets to the MasterVault so that this indivisible share unit is worth slightly more than each bid. Each subsequent match then rounds the purchased shares to zero.

The proof of concept in `test/audit/MatchingEngineZeroShareDrain.t.sol` demonstrates two victims each posting and funding a 1,000,000 USDC bid before the donation. The attacker donates 2,000,000 USDC, causes both bids to be filled for zero shares using the unchanged one-unit ask, and finally redeems that share. The attacker finishes approximately 1,000,000 USDC ahead. The attack requires no malicious Oracle, nonstandard token, or unusual operator-selected partial-fill amount.


#### Recommendation

Reject any match for which the calculated `instrumentAmount` is zero. More generally, after rounding the instrument amount, recompute the asset amount owed for the shares actually transferred instead of consuming the bid's full remaining balance. Leave unspent bid assets in the order or return them to the buyer.

Add regression coverage for zero-share partial fills, high share prices, and below-minimum remainders created from otherwise valid orders.

#### Developer Response

The zero-output loss path is addressed in https://github.com/tenka-fi/10k/pull/320, commit [689cf4aa](https://github.com/tenka-fi/10k/commit/689cf4aab1668bc213d2462d103e01a36306f740). Matching now rejects zero instrument or asset amounts before consuming orders or transferring proceeds. Regression tests cover dust remainders and donation-induced zero-share fills.

The same commit adds operator-provided P2P fill limits and independent order completion. The broader recommendation to recompute payment after nonzero share rounding is not implemented.
